+/*
+ * GW2 on Mac heat probes and opt-in tweaks for one process (WINE_HEAT_*)
+ *
+ * Everything here is off unless one of these is set and the process's exe (the last part of
+ * its ImagePathName) is WINE_HEAT_EXE, default Gw2-64.exe; every other process decides "off"
+ * on its first probe:
+ *
+ *   WINE_HEAT_COUNT=1       per-thread counters of waits, sleeps, yields, event and server
+ *                           calls, and the game's callers of the busiest ones, dumped every
+ *                           WINE_HEAT_INTERVAL seconds (default 5) to
+ *                           $WINE_HEAT_LOG/heat-<pid>.log (default /tmp)
+ *   WINE_HEAT_EVPOOL=1      NtClose of an unnamed, non-inheritable EVENT_ALL_ACCESS event that
+ *                           was never duplicated or handed to the server (async I/O, registry
+ *                           notification) parks the handle instead of closing it, and
+ *                           NtCreateEvent of the same kind reuses one parked for at least
+ *                           WINE_HEAT_EVPOOL_AGE_US (default 2000) microseconds: no server
+ *                           round trips, and no fd_cache_mutex, for create, close and the
+ *                           first set or wait of each new handle
+ *   WINE_HEAT_QOS=1=utility a thread that sets its own base priority (SetThreadPriority on
+ *                           GetCurrentThread()) to one listed gets that macOS scheduling
+ *                           class instead of Wine's mapping: utility (base priority 20: the
+ *                           P cores, as utility QoS), default (31), background (Darwin
+ *                           background, throttled) or a base priority; a comma-separated
+ *                           list, e.g. "1=utility,-1=24"
+ *
+ *   WINE_HEAT_WAKE=one      a semaphore release of count n wakes at most n waiters, one wake
+ *                           syscall each, stopping at the first that finds none (msync wakes
+ *                           all of them on every release)
+ *   WINE_HEAT_WAKE=skip     a release of a semaphore only this process can wait on, with no
+ *                           thread asleep on it, makes no wake syscall; "one,skip" for both
+ *   WINE_HEAT_PROF=on       ~1 kHz SIGPROF samples of the game thread's x86 registers and PE
+ *                           stack to $WINE_HEAT_LOG/heat-prof-<pid>.bin (WINE_HEAT_PROF_HZ,
+ *                           WINE_HEAT_PROF_RENDER=1 for bgfx's render thread too)
+ *   WINE_HEAT_PROF_THREADS=game,render,encode,finish  the sampled threads (default game):
+ *                           bgfx's render thread and DXMT's encode and finish threads by name
+ *                           ("dxmt" = encode,finish; "all"); in Wine's unix code also the unix
+ *                           frame-pointer chain, named with dladdr in heat-prof-<pid>.usyms
+ *   WINE_HEAT_CONTROL=<file> read every second: "wake=…", "count=on|off", "prof=on|off",
+ *                           "targets=<as WINE_HEAT_PROF_THREADS>" switch
+ *                           at runtime (the skip's bookkeeping then runs from the start)
+ *   WINE_HEAT_SPIN=lock=<us>[:game|:all],event=<us>[:game|:all]  (round 7) spin up to that
+ *                           long before sleeping (1-1000 us):
+ *                           lock: in NtWaitForAlertByThreadId (contended critical sections, SRW
+ *                           locks, WaitOnAddress, condition variables); while it spins the
+ *                           thread's alert word holds 3, so NtAlertThreadByThreadId (unchanged)
+ *                           sees a nonzero word and makes no wake syscall. event: in an msync
+ *                           wait whose objects are all events (GW2's job-group waits; never a
+ *                           semaphore, so idle job workers don't spin). Per thread and kind,
+ *                           after 4 misses in a row the thread doesn't spin for its next
+ *                           WINE_HEAT_SPIN_PAUSE (default 32) waits; a hit resets the count.
+ *                           Scope "game" (the default): a thread that set its own priority above
+ *                           normal (GW2's job workers, ABOVE_NORMAL) never spins; the game thread
+ *                           and every other normal-priority thread do. "all": every thread. Spin
+ *                           loops use YieldProcessor() (pause), as RtlEnterCriticalSection does.
+ *   WINE_HEAT_LAZYCLOCK=1   (round 7) an msync wait reads the clock only when it has to turn a
+ *                           relative timeout into a deadline (athei/wine 7134955a, that part only)
+ *   WINE_HEAT_MSYNC_UNREG=1 (round 7) when a multiple-object wait finds an object signaled
+ *                           right after registering with the server, it unregisters (server
+ *                           message, and the alert object's count too) instead of only dropping
+ *                           the objects' local counts (dappermint/winecx 9be392b3b4)
+ *   WINE_HEAT_MSYNC_PORTFIX=1 (round 7) msync's shared-memory page request releases its reply
+ *                           port's receive right, not only the send right (athei/wine 7134955a)
+ *   WINE_HEAT_QOS=1=utility:3  (round 7) only the first 3 threads that set that priority get
+ *                           the class; the others keep Wine's mapping
+ *   WINE_HEAT_NAMEPRI=<name>=<class>;...  a thread that names itself <name>
+ *                           (SetThreadDescription) gets that class or base priority, as
+ *                           WINE_HEAT_QOS; e.g. "bgfx - renderer backend thread=35"
+ *   WINE_HEAT_METAL_HUD=1   sets MTL_HUD_ENABLED=1 in this process's unix environment, unless
+ *                           it is already set: the Metal HUD on the game only, not on Steam.
+ *                           Metal reads it once, at the process's first device, and winemac
+ *                           creates that device before d3d11/dxgi load, so it can't be done there
+ *                           (jdrly/dxmt #138). The Windows environment isn't changed: the game's
+ *                           own child processes don't inherit it.
+ *
+ * See gw2-on-mac docs/research/heat-workers-2026-10-28.md and heat-locks-2026-10-29.md.
